WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.060
Good morning Hank, it’s Tuesday.

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:03.600
Oh look it’s Hank’s book an

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.710
Absolutely Remarkable Thing
which he wants you to know is

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:07.500
available at fine book stores everywhere.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:08.780
Has been since 2018.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.800
Hank, today we’re doing
something called Mindblowers

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.960
with Hank and John where we
share with each other the

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.180
most mind blowing facts
that we know.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.180
Oh ok.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:17.699
Ok Hank here’s my first fact.

00:00:17.699 --> 00:00:19.980
Pi calculated to the 38th

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.140
digit is precise enough to calculate,
assuming that we knew all the

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.259
other numbers, the size of the Milky
Way galaxy to within a

00:00:29.259 --> 00:00:31.429
few centimeters.

00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:35.270
So like we only
need to know pi to the 38th digit,

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:39.530
but we know pi
to the 31 trillionth digit.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:42.510
That’s not a fact about pi,
it’s a fact about humans.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:43.510
We’re amazing.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.600
I can’t get enough of us.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.170
I read a few years ago that
there are probably fragments

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:49.851
of dinosaur bone on the moon.

00:00:49.851 --> 00:00:50.851
The asteroid that killed the

00:00:50.851 --> 00:00:53.560
dinosaurs probably shed debris
as far as Jupiter.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.240
Another great mind blowing
fact is that science communicators

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.970
figured out that bananas were
a really good system

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.190
for the scale of radiation.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:03.310
How so?

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.619
They have a little bit of
radiation in them, so eating

00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:08.369
one banana exposes you
to some radiation so you

00:01:08.369 --> 00:01:11.689
want to know how much
radiation you get flying in a

00:01:11.689 --> 00:01:14.429
jet airplane to Europe,
you can do it in bananas.

00:01:14.429 --> 00:01:16.880
Now I’m going to have google
how many bananas an

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.700
airplane flight is to find
out if it was worth

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.280
it to come here to visit you.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.280
Here’s a mind blower that

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.330
Hank taught me: before the
big bang is an incorrect idea

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:30.399
because in addition to
creating space as we now

00:01:30.399 --> 00:01:33.149
understand it, the Big
Bang created time as

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:36.319
we currently understand it.

00:01:36.319 --> 00:01:37.569
To me, the mindblowing fact

00:01:37.569 --> 00:01:40.929
is that you can look up into
the sky with a telescope

00:01:40.929 --> 00:01:42.979
and see the Big Bang.

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:44.649
This is a great one, Henry

00:01:44.649 --> 00:01:45.649
Reich told me this.

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:47.759
If the
universe was infinitely big

00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:50.840
and infinitely old, the night
sky would be white.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:51.840
Why?

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.920
Because there would be
light from all of the stars in

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.069
every direction, there
would be no space not

00:01:57.069 --> 00:01:59.789
taken up by stars because
the universe is infinitely big

00:01:59.789 --> 00:02:01.759
and infinitely old.

00:02:01.759 --> 00:02:03.159
[Mindblown]

00:02:03.159 --> 00:02:04.340
Ok Hank, here’s one
that gets me.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:05.909
For 99.99%

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:09.030
of human history you
could only hear music if you

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:12.480
were within earshot of
someone who was making it.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.890
And the idea of an album
of recorded music is

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:20.310
younger than Dr. Pepper.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.010
We do not know why
there is matter.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:24.010
What?

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.840
And we do not know, like
in two different ways:

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:27.840
1.)

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.460
we don’t know why there
wasn’t an equal amount

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.450
of antimatter and matter
created and so everything

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:32.450
annihilated itself.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:36.190
Like the big
bang should have been like, even Steven.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.490
It big bangs and
then it big smoops.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:42.620
Well then, space and time
continue to bang, but matter does not.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.072
According to our
understanding of the universe,

00:02:44.072 --> 00:02:47.590
a universe without matter
is just as likely if not more

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:49.260
likely than a universe with matter.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.260
And then separately

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.650
from that, we don’t know why
matter exists at all.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:56.290
So we don’t really
know why I’m here.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.870
Oh, definitely not.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.790
Like on way more levels than just those two.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:02.680
Right like we don’t know
why I’m here, in the sense

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:06.210
that we don’t know why the
atoms that are inside of me exist.

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:07.210
Yeah.

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:10.130
It would be interesting to
look at what makes a fact mind blowing.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.650
I think it’s when it makes me
feel either big or small.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.550
When it recontextualizes my
understanding of myself and my

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:17.810
place in the universe.

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.180
Yeah, like I
want to understand my own

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:23.880
emotions because ultimately
I am the most interesting part of the universe.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:27.730
I would argue that I am.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.710
It’s weird, do you not wake
up in the morning and your

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.950
first thought is of me
and my needs?

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:36.740
Some days when it’s a really
bad day, I do think, well we are

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:39.320
on an infinitely tiny speck.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:40.320
Yeah.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.960
On the one hand, it’s that feeling of

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:44.040
wonderful smallness.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.050
Which you
would think would be sad, but isn’t.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.150
But on the other hand, there’s the
feeling of like, well there might

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:50.560
not have been matter.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.120
So in that
sense I am quite something.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.030
This whole thing is weird.

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:58.460
Thank you for coming to my TED talk.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:00.120
Hank I’ll continue to see you right now.

